#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""needs-you-expiry: a card before a TLS certificate, a domain or an API key expires.

  needs-you-expiry [--dry-run] [--verbose] [--state PATH]

Run it once a day (cron, a systemd user timer or a LaunchAgent) on one machine. It does
nothing until you list what to watch in ~/.config/needs-you/expiry.conf, one per line:

  tls     example.com                    # the certificate served on port 443
  tls     mail.example.com:993           # any port that speaks TLS from the first byte
  domain  example.com                    # the registration's expiry, from RDAP
  key     "Figma PAT"  2026-12-31        # a date you know: the day it stops working
  key     "Deploy key" 2027-03-01  renew=https://example.com/settings/keys  context=personal

Options on any line: renew=<https URL> (the card's link), context=work|personal; on a
tls line, verify=no reads the date of a self-signed or private-CA certificate.

Each run reads every date, then for each thing: no card while the date is further away
than the largest threshold; inside it, one card (key <context>:expiry:<kind>:<name>) whose
priority rises as the date nears; resolved when the date moves out of the window (you
renewed) or the line goes away. A check that fails (host unreachable, RDAP error) keeps
the last date it read; once any check has failed NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_FAILS runs in a row,
one low card per machine lists the failing checks. It always exits 0.

Certificates are verified (system CAs, host name): one that doesn't verify is a failing
check, except an expired one, which is re-read unverified to date it. Nothing is sent to
the host but a TLS handshake. Domains
are looked up over HTTPS RDAP: the server for the TLD comes from IANA's bootstrap file
(https://data.iana.org/rdap/dns.json), cached for a week next to the state.

Config (environment, else ~/.config/needs-you/env; all optional):
  NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_CONFIG    the list (default: expiry.conf next to the env file)
  NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_DAYS      thresholds, days=priority (default 30=low,7=normal,1=urgent);
                             expired is always urgent
  NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_CONTEXT   work or personal (default NEEDS_YOU_DEFAULT_CONTEXT, else work)
  NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_FAILS     failed runs in a row before the failing-checks card (default 2)
  NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_INTERVAL  hours between runs (default 24); cards expire after 3x this
  NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_MAX_CARDS at most this many expiry cards open at once (default 20)
  NEEDS_YOU_EXPIRY_TIMEOUT   seconds per check (default 10)
  NEEDS_YOU_BIN              path to the needs-you CLI (default: on PATH, else ~/.local/bin)

State: ~/.local/state/needs-you/expiry.json (mode 600): what it posted, the last dates.
Python 3.9+ standard library only.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import calendar
import datetime
import hashlib
